<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/advanced/brain-imaging-methods">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cognition leaves no fossil record; it must be measured live. Every brain-imaging method is a
    bargain struck between physical constraints, and the single most useful sentence in this chapter
    is: <strong>no method gets space and time together</strong>. fMRI sees millimetre voxels a second
    late; EEG sees millisecond dynamics through a blur; lesions tell you what a region is
    <em>for</em> but only after it is gone; stimulation tells you what happens when you intervene but
    coarse. Methodological literacy is knowing which axis your question lives on — and knowing how
    much statistical machinery stands between raw signal and published claim.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the imaging toolbox and its honest limits" widget="equationStep" config={imagingCfg} />

  <h2>fMRI: the workhorse and its proxy problem</h2>
  <p>
    Functional MRI does not measure neurons. It measures the <strong>BOLD</strong> (blood-oxygen-level
    dependent) contrast: active tissue consumes oxygen, local flow rises to overcompensate, and the
    changing ratio of oxygenated to deoxygenated haemoglobin alters local magnetisation (deoxygenated
    haemoglobin is paramagnetic — the same physics the medical-imaging lesson used). The haemodynamic
    response function peaks roughly 4–6 seconds after neural firing and decays over ~12; experiments
    therefore use block or rapid event-related designs and a general linear model that convolves the
    predicted neural timecourse with the HRF, testing each voxel for condition-related variance.
    Three consequences to internalise. First, temporal resolution is capped at ~1 second even though
    the scanner samples faster — the blood, not the machine, is the bottleneck. Second, the signal is
    a <em>net</em> of excitation, inhibition and local field potentials; "activation" means increased
    metabolic demand, not "more neurons firing" (inhibitory interneurons consume oxygen too). Third,
    the whole edifice rests on neurovascular coupling staying calibrated — which varies with age,
    drugs, and disease, a confound fMRI inherits silently.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the brain region for X lit up." A blob on a statistical map is a
    test statistic thresholded over ~100,000 voxels with massive multiple-comparison correction, not
    a spotlight on a faculty. The stronger fallacy is <strong>reverse inference</strong>: concluding
    the mental state from the activation. If a region activates during fear, but also during novelty,
    arousal, faces and reward, then activation is weak evidence for fear in particular. Legitimate
    reverse inference exists only with quantified selectivity — activation-pattern overlap rates
    across a large task library — which is rarely what pop-neuroscience headlines have done.
  </p>

  <h2>EEG and MEG: time resolved, space blurred</h2>
  <p>
    Electroencephalography records the summed postsynaptic currents of synchronised cortical
    populations, through skull and scalp, at sub-millisecond resolution. Two analysis families:
    <strong>event-related potentials</strong> — averaging epochs time-locked to stimuli exposes
    components with functional identities (N170 for face processing, mismatch negativity for
    auditory surprise, P300 for context updating, lateralised readiness potential preceding voluntary
    movement — Soon and colleagues could decode movement choice from prefrontal-parietal activity
    several seconds before subjects reported deciding, a finding that launched a decade of
    free-will neuroimaging) — and <strong>oscillations</strong>, where band-limited power (theta,
    alpha, beta, gamma) indexes arousal, attention and binding hypotheses. The limitation is the
    <strong>inverse problem</strong>: infinitely many 3-D current configurations produce the same
    scalp voltage distribution, so source localisation is model-dependent guesswork (magneto-
    encephalography, measuring magnetic fields, suffers less skull distortion but shares the
    fundamental ambiguity). The productive division of labour: EEG tells you <em>when</em>, fMRI
    tells you <em>roughly where</em>, and combined EEG-fMRI studies exploit the complement.
  </p>

  <h2>Lesions and stimulation: the causal arm</h2>
  <p>
    Correlation cannot establish that a region is <em>necessary</em>; a lesion can. The classical
    logic is the <strong>double dissociation</strong>: patient A with damage to region R1 loses
    function X but not Y, patient B with damage to R2 loses Y but not X — evidence that X and Y are
    distinct systems with distinct substrates (Broca's expressive aphasia versus Wernicke's receptive
    aphasia was the founding case; H.M. versus K.F. separated long-term episodic memory from intact
    digit span). Caveats: lesions are uncontrolled in size and location, the brain reorganises
    (especially over years), and a spared function after damage does not prove the region is
    irrelevant (compensation). <strong>Transcranial magnetic stimulation</strong> imports the lesion
    logic into healthy volunteers — a brief magnetic pulse transiently disrupts a targeted patch
    (virtual lesion) or, at lower intensity, probes corticospinal excitability with millisecond
    timing; it is the closest humans get to causal, reversible, within-subject perturbation, with
    the standing caveats of coarse focality and stimulation depth. Optogenetics completes the causal
    toolkit in animal models: genetically targeted, light-activated opsins let experimenters excite
    or silence defined cell classes on millisecond timescales — the difference between watching the
    engine and controlling the throttle.
  </p>

  <h2>The statistical reckoning</h2>
  <p>
    Neuroimaging's data are enormous (a scanning session is hundreds of thousands of voxels by
    hundreds of timepoints) and its samples historically small (early fMRI studies: 8–20 subjects),
    so the field's crisis was predictable and instructive. The <strong>dead salmon</strong> study
    (2009) ran a standard pipeline on a scanned Atlantic salmon shown emotional faces and "found"
    activation in its brain cavity — by skipping multiple-comparison correction; the Ig-Nobel-winning
    point was that uncorrected voxelwise statistics manufacture signal from noise. Eklund and
    colleagues (2016) then showed that a widely used clusterwise correction (AFNI's default) had a
    parametric assumption violated by real data, inflating significance across a decade of published
    results. Vul and colleagues (2009) attacked the other flank: "voodoo correlations" in social
    neuroscience where overlapping analytic selections inflated brain-behaviour correlations. The
    corrective practices are now mainstream and non-negotiable in serious work: family-wise error
    control or false-discovery-rate correction, preregistration, independent validation samples, data
    sharing (OpenNeuro), and scale — UK Biobank and the Human Connectome Project run thousands of
    subjects, shifting the field's estimates toward their true (usually smaller) sizes.
  </p>

  <h2>Structural imaging and morphometry</h2>
  <p>
    Anatomy is the base layer. Structural MRI yields voxel volumes, cortical thickness and
    surface area; morphometric analysis across subjects maps development (grey-matter
    maturation through adolescence), aging and disease signatures (hippocampal atrophy in
    Alzheimer's, cortical thinning in schizophrenia). The statistical machinery — voxel-based
    morphometry, cortical surface pipelines — carries the same inference caveats as fMRI:
    multiple comparisons, site effects (scanner drift is measurable and correctable), and
    effect sizes that shrink as samples grow. The ENIGMA consortium's answer is scale —
    harmonised meta-analysis across dozens of sites and tens of thousands of subjects, which
    set the field's current standards for what counts as a structural difference. The honest
    summary: group-level structural differences are real and reliable; single-subject "brain
    age" or diagnostic claims remain far weaker than commercial marketing suggests.
  </p>

  <h2>PET, SPECT, and the molecular imaging of cognition</h2>
  <p>
    Nuclear methods image molecules, and their neuroscience applications are distinct from
    anatomy: FDG-PET maps glucose metabolism (diagnostic patterns across dementia subtypes);
    amyloid and tau PET tracers visualise Alzheimer's pathology <em>in vivo</em> — the
    biomarker revolution that redefined trial enrolment (pathology-positive, preclinical
    participants) and turned the amyloid hypothesis into a testable longitudinal question;
    dopamine D2/D3 tracers exposed relationships between striatal receptor availability and
    obesity, addiction and impulsivity. SPECT's cheaper perfusion imaging persists in cardiac
    and psychiatric use. The costs: radiation, tracers measured in half-lives, and the same
    proxy problem as fMRI in a different chemistry — molecular imaging measures binding,
    occupancy and metabolism, from which function is inferred. When the two proxy systems
    (haemodynamic and molecular) agree, the inference is stronger than either alone — a
    principle the field applies increasingly systematically.
  </p>

  <h2>The ethics layer: privacy, prediction, responsibility</h2>
  <p>
    Imaging's predictive power raises the normative questions before the accuracy justifies
    them. <strong>Neural privacy</strong>: decoded content (what a person is perceiving or
    intending) remains crude outside constrained tasks, but the trajectory is one-directional,
    and "cognitive liberty" proposals (neurorights legislation pioneered in Chile) respond to
    the trajectory, not the moment. <strong>Prediction in law</strong>: brain-based evidence
    has entered sentencing (adolescent-development arguments, tumour-defence cases) where the
    science supports group-level claims poorly suited to individual verdicts — the
    ecological-fallacy problem inside a courtroom. <strong>Responsibility</strong>: findings
    that "the brain did it" distinguish nothing — every choice has neural correlates — and
    neurolaw's careful literature keeps separating mitigation evidence from exculpation. The
    discipline's standing contribution is calibration: stating precisely how weak the current
    claims are is itself the ethical practice.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>From blobs to information.</strong> Univariate activation asks "is this region busier?";
    <strong>multivoxel pattern analysis (MVPA)</strong> asks "what information does the spatial
    pattern carry?" — a classifier trained on activity patterns can predict which of several stimuli
    a person viewed, sometimes which memory they were retrieving, with above-chance accuracy. This
    moved neuroimaging from localisation to <em>representation</em>, and sharpened the mental-privacy
    questions (decoding lie, intent, emotion — with accuracy far below the forensic fantasy but above
    chance, and the ethics of consent and coercion riding on that gap). <strong>Connectomics</strong>
    reframed the brain as a network: diffusion MRI tractography maps white-matter edges, resting-state
    correlations define functional networks (the default-mode network's discovery by observing what
    the brain does when "doing nothing" is the field's most-cited side effect), and graph measures —
    modularity, hubness, lesion-simulated attack tolerance — connect structure to cognition and
    disease (schizophrenia and Alzheimer's as network disorders). Honest limits: tractography's false
    positives are notorious (crossing fibres), and functional connectivity is statistical association,
    not wiring. <strong>Open questions:</strong> what the neural code actually is (rate, timing,
    population geometry — high-dimensional representational similarity analysis is the current lens);
    how to align brains across individuals whose functional anatomy differs (hyperalignment); whether
    naturalistic paradigms (films, stories) can replace the artificial tasks whose constraints shaped
    a generation of conclusions; and whether BOLD's confound structure can ever be fully modelled —
    or whether the next decade belongs to faster, directer measures (voltage-sensitive dyes,
    calcium imaging at depth, and wearable optical imaging) that finally bring neural time and neural
    space into one experiment.
  </p>
  <p>
    The translational pipeline deserves its own accounting, because imaging is where
    neuroscience meets public claims most often. Educational products claiming to train brains
    or diagnose learning styles by scan rest on group differences of small effect; courtroom
    admissions of neuroimaging outpace individual-level validity; commercial "hiring by
    brainwave" and "lie detection" services sell certainty the underlying studies do not
    support. The professional defence is disciplined translation: report effect sizes rather
    than blob pictures; state classification accuracies with cross-validation and base rates;
    and keep the reference-class problem visible — a group-level difference ("this population's
    average differs") licenses no individual diagnosis ("this person has X") without
    individual-level validation. That gap separates almost all published imaging science from
    almost all marketed imaging products, and the field's credibility is maintained exactly
    here, one calibrated claim at a time.
  </p>
  <p>
    A measurement-science addendum: the field's agreement problem is being industrialised.
    Consensus pipelines (harmonised acquisition protocols, phantom-based calibration,
    travelling-subject studies that scan the same people across sites) quantify scanner
    variability directly; open-source analysis containers make pipelines reproducible rather
    than mythical; and registered reports separate design approval from results, immunising
    against the analytic garden of forking paths. None of this is glamorous, and all of it is
    why the current decade's imaging findings are smaller, sturdier and more cumulative than
    the previous one's — the quiet institutional progress that makes the exciting claims
    above checkable at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, the comparison with the animal toolkit is the honest frame for every human
    claim: what imaging infers through proxies, invasive methods measure directly — single
    neurons, defined circuits, causal perturbations. The translation between the two is the
    field's ongoing work, and the researcher's first question when evaluating any human
    neuroimaging claim is which animal-model finding it should be answerable to.
  </p>

